#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110368 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/id #uuid "4a8f8deb-2ff1-56d2-888a-3e51eb805bc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-17212" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(5s + 4)x = 9x\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 5s + 4, \\qquad b = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5s + 4 = 9.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5s & =5 \\\\ s & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110371 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "6eeec12a-5da8-5113-916a-58f4ca807207" :question/math-academy-id "e-18852" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n3r = 12.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k = 2$ and $r = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + r = 2+4 = 6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110372 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "6355ac1e-bb4d-5133-9917-04dfb2bc5a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18854" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$\n\n- $-3x = -4x + 6$\n\n- $2x + 7 = 2x - 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =4x+1 \\\\ 4x+1-4x & =4x+1-4x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x & =-4x+6 \\\\ -3x+4x & =-4x+6+4x \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-3x = -4x + 6$ has the unique solution $x = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+7 & =2x-1 \\\\ 2x+7-2x & =2x-1-2x \\\\ 7 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26fe00c2-ff7d-5b62-96ab-0af209fd24e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295480} {:answer/id #uuid "389f9ac4-b9fe-5bf6-9689-5a77751235bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295586} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd45365-694b-59a1-9c75-444ba21c1ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295949} {:answer/id #uuid "bc73343c-08ba-5843-8a40-f11269cd8d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296258} {:answer/id #uuid "ff514a4f-a0d5-5889-8487-a38b01139445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cd45365-694b-59a1-9c75-444ba21c1ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295949} :answer-field/id #uuid "632eea42-d449-505d-9c6f-a3dc34d529ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e80edf76-d1e3-576c-b5c7-753b9159f67b" :question/math-academy-id "q-288440" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n-4kx=8x\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-4k,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n-4k=8.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11383c9f-6bca-51a0-bf87-839bec637ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11383c9f-6bca-51a0-bf87-839bec637ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295365} :answer-field/id #uuid "b78bfb02-4c20-5a75-811c-305086ea1983" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73256cc5-b18c-5ffc-bb1b-9018e84f6eb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-288583" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ and $m$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t+m?$\n\n$t+m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $t$ and $m$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n1-m=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $t=3$ and $m=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\nt+m=3+(-3)=0.\n$$"}]}
